/tests/test_unanswered_calls.py", "packages/evals/tests/test_unreadable_run_refusals.py", "packages/evals/tests/test_untyped_measure_declarations.py", "packages/evals/tests/test_usage_capture.py", "packages/evals/tests/test_viz_timeseries.py", "packages/evals/tests/test_wall_clock_ceilings.py", "packages/evals/tests/test_weakest_margin_and_reader_names.py", "packages/evals/tests/test_whole_run_reads_paged.py", "packages/evals/tests/test_witnessed_cell.py", "packages/evals/tests/test_witnessed_judging.py", "packages/evals/tests/test_writer_prompt_retired.py", "packages/evals/tests/toyhost_plan.py", "packages/registry/tests/unit/registry/test_unsigned_agent_concession_expires.py", "tests/enforcement/_model_literal_exemptions.txt", "tests/enforcement/_public_api_consumers.txt", "tests/enforcement/test_a_tool_states_its_notes_first.py"], "files_oracle": [], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "blocking", "title": "Test failures in the recorded evidence: two reds at d642ea2be", "recommendation": "Instance. The pool log .prawduct/pool-kairo-d642ea2be.txt records 2 failed out of 50,757. (1) tests/enforcement/test_api_growth_requires_a_minor_bump.py::test_the_workspace_declares_an_honest_version (0.85.0 < v0.86.0), left to the maintainer by owner ruling, which the plan records under 'Version red'. (2) packages/evals/tests/test_plan_dataset.py::test_the_kairo_bundles_report_input_matches_r5_on_every_field_r5_carries. This delta caused it: shape 65 moves the qwen3.8-flash/passed contrast interval (built -0.1285, r5 holds -0.1259, plus 7 more items). It ships red by an owner ruling and gets a follow-up recording after 00:00 UTC 2026-11-01. Under protocol, a failure in the evidence is BLOCKING whatever the ruling. Record both as accepted dispositions, citing each ruling and the follow-up for (2), so the ruling sits on the record. Do not weaken either test.", "files": ["packages/evals/tests/test_plan_dataset.py", "tests/enforcement/test_api_growth_requires_a_minor_bump.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "blocking", "title": "A boolean guardrail is now decided on its rate per case, with no test", "recommendation": "Instance. Before f1eef950e a boolean measure had no per-case value, so `_multiple_comparisons` skipped any guardrail check on a boolean guardrail (its `if values[...].get(reading)` filter). `_per_case_values` now emits `_pooled_rates`, and `_guardrail_readings` reads `_measure_range` (0 to 1), so every boolean declared `guardrail=True` gets a `GuardrailCheck` with a held, breached or undecided decision. CHANGELOG and MIGRATIONS state this ('A boolean guardrail is held on its rate per case too'). No test builds a boolean guardrail: test_boolean_contrasts.py covers only family comparisons and planning, and test_guardrails.py uses numeric guardrails. Add a test that builds a directed boolean guardrail and asserts its check's per-case samples (the shares), its interval on the 0 to 1 range, and its decision against its materiality margin.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/bundle/comparisons.py", "packages/evals/tests/test_boolean_contrasts.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "test-status: no recorded test evidence on disk", "recommendation": "Instance. `prawduct-hook test-status` returned 'stale: no .test-evidence.json on disk' (exit 1). The only evidence is the builder's pool log. Record evidence for d642ea2be through the evidence store before the PR, so freshness is machine-checked and not just claimed.", "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "_is_rate's check branch (a directed boolean, a pass_fail measure) is drawn on the bounded interval with no assertion pinning it", "recommendation": "Instance. Of `_is_rate`'s branches, test_rate_contrast_bounded.py pins the trial and goal_state branches (test_every_rate_in_the_family_follows_its_bounded_interval) and the judged branch. test_report_decision_rule.py pins accuracy. Nothing asserts `interval == bounded_interval` for a directed boolean or a host measure declared `pass_fail`. test_boolean_contrasts.py asserts only verdict 'regressed', which the t interval would also give. Deleting `catalog[name].is_check` from `_is_rate` would likely leave every test green, so owner ruling (c)'s 'wide' set is not fully held by a test. Add assertions on top_up_never (interval equals bounded_interval) and on a pass_fail measure comparison, and add the mutation to the file's mutation list.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/bundle/comparisons.py", "packages/evals/tests/test_rate_contrast_bounded.py", "packages/evals/tests/test_boolean_contrasts.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "severity": "warning", "title": "The build plan does not carry the delta's three owner-ruled changes or the shape bump, and its constraint contradicts them", "recommendation": "Instance. build-plan-kairo-report-gaps.md scopes five gaps (chunks A to E). Its 'Decisions made mid-build' does not list the directed-boolean contrasts, the late-criterion naming, the paired-cases basis wording, or the rates-on-bounded-interval rule with its bundle shape 64 to 65. Its Constraints still say 'Old snapshot digests and recorded fixtures still match', but shape 65 moves every aspect's fingerprint and evidence digest (test_declared_names.py re-pins them) and re-recorded llm_analysis and prototype5/kairo. The rulings are cited in the CHANGELOG, MIGRATIONS.toml and the schema shape comment, so they are not undocumented. The plan, though, describes work narrower than what shipped. Add the three rulings, the shape bump, and the known-red r5 follow-up to the plan's mid-build decisions, and amend the constraint to name the owner-ruled exception.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-kairo-report-gaps.md"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "note", "title": "A rate that the t test separates but whose bounded interval reaches zero reads not_separated with no reason", "recommendation": "Instance. In `_corrected_family`, when `p_adjusted < alpha` and the rate's bounded interval reaches zero (or is None), the verdict becomes 'not_separated' but `not_separated_reason` stays as `_compare` set it (usually None). The report input's `Contrast.reason` is then empty beside an adjusted p under alpha. A writer sees p_adjusted 0.01 and 'not separated' with nothing to explain it. The rerecorded fixtures hold no such case ('every verdict holding'), so nothing is wrong today. Set a reason naming the bounded interval when this rule is what withholds the separation.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/bundle/comparisons.py"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "note", "title": "migrate_bundle_shape_65.py cannot faithfully migrate a bundle holding a directed boolean on a merit axis", "recommendation": "Instance. The script says shape 65 'changes one thing over unchanged evidence'. But the same release adds a comparison for each directed boolean on a merit axis, which widens the family and moves every adjusted p in it. Migrating a shape-64 bundle with such a measure gives a shape-65 bundle missing those comparisons, and the stray-path guard cannot catch it because it only looks for fields that changed, not ones that should have been added. The Kairo round-2 bundle has no boolean in its catalog, so the committed fixture is sound. Have the script refuse a bundle whose measure_catalog holds a boolean with higher_is_better on a merit axis.", "files": ["packages/evals/tests/fixtures/report_plans/migrate_bundle_shape_65.py"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "'Is a rate' has no single source of truth, and the bundle does not record which interval a contrast is drawn on", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: the bounded-interval rule was added as a new predicate beside the rate sets that already existed, and none of them was made to read another. Shape 65 promises that a floor's or a campaign reading's interval on a rate nests inside its contrast's (reading-reports.md, MIGRATIONS.toml, the _corrected_family docstring). That holds only while these separate definitions agree: (1) comparisons._is_rate (trial, judged range == (0,1), is_check, goal_check_of, ACCURACY_MEASURE); (2) the floor's rate set in report/decision.floor_readings (trial, rule_rates over is_check minus classifier labels, the goal-check special case inside _held, a judged dimension with range (0,1) on the capability axis, match read on accuracy via _compared_as); (3) kernel/declaration.decision_readings (template goal checks, pass_fail capability dimensions, rule_rates); (4) a copied _is_rate in tests/fixtures/report_plans/migrate_bundle_shape_65.py, which works on raw dicts. _is_rate's docstring defines itself as 'the readings a floor reads as pass rates' but never calls that code. On top of that, the method an interval was drawn by is not stored: FamilyComparison.basis names the test the p came from, so scorecard._reconciled has to infer 'drawn on bounded' from `comparison.interval == comparison.bounded_interval`. If a new rate kind (another pass/fail scale, a new check family) is added to one list and not the others, the Contrasts table draws it on a t interval while the floor reads it bounded. That is exactly the split the docs say cannot happen, and no test catches it: test_rate_contrast_bounded covers trial, goal checks and a pass/fail judged dimension on hand-built shapes, not 'every floor rate is a bounded contrast'. Fix with one construction: put a public predicate in kernel.metrics (or the bundle) and have floor_readings, decision_readings, _is_rate and the migration script all read it. Also record the interval's method on FamilyComparison (for example interval_basis: t | bounded). Shape 65 is not tagged yet, so the field costs no extra bump, and consumers would read it instead of comparing tuples. Alternatively, add a test that, over a shape with every rate kind, asserts each FloorReadings rate's family comparison has interval == bounded_interval.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/bundle/comparisons.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/decision.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/kernel/declaration.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/campaign/scorecard.py", "packages/evals/tests/fixtures/report_plans/migrate_bundle_shape_65.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/bundle/schema.py"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "A rate whose adjusted p clears alpha but whose bounded interval reaches zero reads not_separated with no reason", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why it broke: the verdict now has two gates from two different methods (the t test's Holm p and the bounded interval), but _corrected_family records why only for the p gate. This used to be rare, limited to Holm's later steps; it is now the ordinary result for a rate on few cases (test_a_rate_the_t_interval_separates_and_the_bounded_one_does_not_is_not_separated pins one). The comparison carries p_adjusted < alpha, verdict not_separated, and not_separated_reason None. Downstream: Contrast.reason (report_input_build.py:837) is empty; Verdict.reason is 'no_margin' or 'not_inside_margin' (report/build._contrast_reason), which a program branching on the reason cannot tell apart from a p that did not clear; and _contrast_words prints a bare 'not separated' beside a p below alpha. The writer and a host both see a p of, say, 0.004 next to 'not separated', and only the family-wide disclosure sentence hints why. Fix: in _corrected_family, when p_adjusted < alpha and comparison.delta but not permits, set not_separated_reason to one sentence naming the cause: the rate's bounded interval at the family's level reaches zero. It then flows to Contrast.reason, Verdict.reason_detail and the contrast words with no other change. Optionally add a VerdictReason code for it so a program can branch.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/bundle/comparisons.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/build.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/blocks.py"], "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "late_subject recovers a floor's aspect and reading by splitting its composed key, which is ambiguous", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why it broke: the late criterion stores only Floor.key, `{aspect}/{kind}/{reading}`, so a consumer that needs the parts has to parse them back. bundle/schema.py builds LateCriterion(criterion='floor', name=floor.key) at the point where floor.aspect and floor.clause.reading are both in hand, and then report_decision.late_subject undoes the join with name.rsplit('/', 2). Splitting from the end handles a slash in the aspect but not one in the reading. RateDrop.reading for a goal check is the check's verbatim text, and goal_check_measure does not escape '/' (_REFERENCE_ESCAPES covers %, |, { and }). So a floor on a check like `ratio/total >= 0.5` in aspect 'billing' splits into aspect 'billing/rate_drop' and measure 'total >= 0.5', and both the doc page's late line (doc_page/build._late_name) and the analyst fact (_late_touch) name the wrong aspect and measure, which is the very confusion this change set out to remove. Fix: carry the structured parts on the late criterion (an aspect and reading on the bundle's and the report input's LateCriterion, left out when unset), or have late_subject take the Floor. Do not parse the key.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report_decision.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/bundle/schema.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/doc_page/build.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/analyst/facts.py"], "fid": "R-10"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "compare._contrast_cases reimplements the contrast figure-key rule that facts.contrast_key owns", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why it broke: the new cases citation needed a contrast's key stem and copied the rule rather than importing it. analyst/compare.py:_contrast_cases copies the rule 'qualify by the aspect only where the input holds the arm and measure more than once', including the `held` filter, from the public analyst/facts.contrast_key, which documents itself as the key contrasts_list and the editor's caveat facts cite under. compare.py does not import facts, and facts does not import compare, so no cycle stops it using that function. If the keying rule changes, compare cites a contrast.* key that no longer resolves and the compare tool fails on every paired row. Fix: build the stem with contrast_key(report_input, contrast), or move contrast_key next to cite in analyst/cited.py so every analyst module reads one copy.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/analyst/compare.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/analyst/facts.py"], "fid": "R-11"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "Prose still says a contrast's interval is a t interval, or Bonferroni-implied by the p", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: shape 65 changed which interval a rate's contrast prints, and the descriptions that state that fact were updated only in schema.py and the two docs. Members found: stats.interval_permits_separation's docstring ('its interval is Bonferroni's'; 'Where no interval exists (no t ...)'); _corrected_family's paragraph 'An interval that excludes zero has m * p_raw < alpha ... so the rule is Bonferroni's wherever an interval exists', which is false for a rate's bounded interval; the ACCURACY_MEASURE comment in kernel/metrics.py ('A boolean has no per-case mean for a family to test'), contradicted by _pooled_rates; report/decision.py's module doc ('A non-inferiority claim never reads the t interval the page's contrasts print'); scorecard._reconciled_lines's docstring ('a contrast on a t interval (or a bounded one where its values have no spread)'); and figure_keys' independent-basis words to the analyst ('reads wider than a paired interval where they ran the same'), which are no longer guaranteed against a rate's corrected, bounded, paired interval. Search the evals src for 't interval' and 'Bonferroni' wherever a contrast's interval is described. Per prose remedies: delete the claim or make it relational (name the reading kinds that are bounded); do not narrate the change.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/stats.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/bundle/comparisons.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/kernel/metrics.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/decision.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/campaign/scorecard.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/figure_keys.py"], "fid": "R-12"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "What a compare row's interval counts is spread over five fields plus a key-suffix parse", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Borderline unmodeled state. ComparisonRow states its unit through counts, cases, nested, counted_unstated and may_be_narrow, and arms_compare's notes branch on their combinations (for example _MAY_BE_NARROW_UNSTATED only where a row is both may_be_narrow and counted_unstated). plan_page/resolve_changes._paired_cases then rebuilds the same facts by splitting each cited figure's key at its last '.'. Today every combination is derived in the one _row function, so no invalid combination is reachable. If the row gains another unit, one field (for example counted: cases | trials | observations | unstated) would keep the producer and the renderer from drifting. No change is required now.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/analyst/compare.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/plan_page/resolve_changes.py"], "fid": "R-13"}, {"goal": "Records Pass", "severity": "warning", "title": "The handoff and plan stop at 974d5f7f5 and the second known red is recorded nowhere", "recommendation": "Bar: it misleads into action. .prawduct/.handoff-notes.md, which the session's memory says to read after a clear, still says STOPPED AT 974d5f7f5 with '1 accepted red' (the version test). It does not mention the delta: the boolean contrasts, the late-criterion and paired-case wording, the one interval method for rates, or bundle shape 65. It also does not mention the second red, test_plan_dataset::test_the_kairo_bundles_report_input_matches_r5_on_every_field_r5_carries (pool-kairo-d642ea2be.txt: 8 r5 contrast intervals differ). That red is owed a re-record commit after 00:00 UTC 2026-11-01, and the dispatch prompt is the only place that says so: no tracked file, test docstring, CHANGELOG line or handoff line does. An agent resuming from the handoff would read a red pool as a regression, or would leave the dated follow-up undone. The plan has the same gap. Its 'Decisions made mid-build' records the version red but none of the delta's three owner rulings. Its Constraints line 'Old snapshot digests and recorded fixtures still match' is contradicted by the shape-65 bump (every stored shape-64 analysis now needs regeneration), and the plan has no amendment. Scope: instance. Fix: record the r5 red, its owner ruling and its follow-up trigger where the version red is recorded (handoff GATES, and the plan's decisions). Bring STOPPED AT to d642ea2be. Amend the plan's constraint for the shape-65 ruling.", "files": [".prawduct/.handoff-notes.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-kairo-report-gaps.md", "packages/evals/tests/test_plan_dataset.py"], "fid": "R-14"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "The late-criterion and paired-case changes reach consumers with no CHANGELOG or MIGRATIONS entry", "recommendation": "Rulings (a), a directed boolean compared on its rate, and (c), a rate drawn on its bounded interval with shape 65, each have a CHANGELOG section and a MIGRATIONS note that state the downstream impact. Ruling (b), from 4a02b7c9d and c3cfc0292, has neither, yet it changes what consumers see. On a multi-aspect page, or one with more than one late criterion of a kind, the doc page's late-criterion caveat labels now read '<criterion> on <measure> in <aspect>'. arms_compare's output gains a new public field, ComparisonRow.cases, and counted_unstated turns False wherever the contrast states its cases. A paired compare row's basis text now says it is paired over N cases, or unpaired over each side's. The analyst and editor help bytes moved, as test_analyst_report_check re-pins. Discodon is about to regenerate the multi-aspect Kairo pilot report and check it. It will see changed late-criterion lines and compare-basis text, and no record says they were intended. MIGRATIONS records the same kind of change elsewhere ('A host pinning rendered page text ... sees the change'). Scope: instance. Fix: add an Unreleased CHANGELOG section and a MIGRATIONS note for (b), naming the new field, the counted_unstated change and the rendered-text change.", "files": ["CHANGELOG.md", "packages/evals/MIGRATIONS.toml", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/analyst/compare.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/doc_page/build.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/plan_page/resolve_changes.py"], "fid": "R-15"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Mid-build decisions in the unreviewed delta, for the owner", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Decisions in the delta: (1) A directed boolean on a merit axis joins its family, tested on its per-case rate. The owner ruled this on 2026-10-31 (MIGRATIONS records it), and the shape does not move for